"A Type and Scope Safe Universe of Syntaxes with Binding: Their Semantics and Proofs (https://bentnib.org/binding-universe.pdf)\nciteturn98view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 30; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://bentnib.org/binding-universe.pdf\",\"lineno\":1180}); Total lines: 1363\nL1125@P25: due to the simplicity of this example: the first refl witnesses the fact that both definitions pick the\nL1126@P25: same constructor (a cons cell), the second that they carry the same natural number, and we can\nL1136@P25: The representation of variable binding in formal systems has been a hot topic for decades. Part of\nL1137@P25: the purpose of the first POPLMark challenge (2005) was to explore and compare various methods.\nL1138@P25: Having based our work on a de Bruijn encoding of variables, and thus a canonical treatment of\nL1139@P25: α-equivalence classes, our work has no direct comparison with permutation-based treatments such\nL1140@P25: as those of Pitts’ and Gabbay’s nominal syntax [Gabbay and Pitts 2001].\nL1141@P25: Our generic universe of syntax is based on scope-and-typed de Bruijn indices [de Bruijn 1972] but\nL1142@P25: it is not a necessity. It is for instance possible to give an interpretation of Descriptions corresponding\nL1143@P25: to Chlipala’s Parametric Higher-Order Abstract Syntax (2008) and we would be interested to see\nL1144@P25: what the appropriate notion of Semantics is for this representation.\nL1145@P25: 9.2 Alternative Binding Structures\nL1146@P25: The binding structure we present here is based on a flat, lexical scoping strategy. There are other\nL1147@P25: strategies and it would be interesting to see whether our approach could be reused in these cases.\nL1148@P25: Bach Poulsen, Rouvoet, Tolmach, Krebbers and Visser (2018) introduce notions of scope graphs\nL1149@P25: and frames to scale the techniques typical of well scoped and typed deep embeddings to imperative\nL1150@P25: languages. They can already handle a large subset of Middleweight Java.\nL1151@P25: We have demonstrated how to write generic programs over the potentially cyclic structures\nL1152@P25: of Ghani, Hamana, Uustalu and Vene (2006). Further work by Hamana (2009) yielded a different\nL1153@P25: presentation of cyclic structures which preserves sharing: pointers can not only refer to nodes\nL1154@P25: above them but also across from them in the cyclic tree. Capturing this class of inductive types as a\nL1155@P25: set of syntaxes with binding and writing generic programs over them is still an open problem.\nL1156@P25: 9.3 Semantics of Syntaxes with Binding\nL1157@P25: An early foundational study of a general semantic framework for signatures with binding, algebras\nL1158@P25: for such signatures, and initiality of the term algebra, giving rise to a categorical ‘program’ for\nL1159@P25: substitution and proofs of its properties, was given by Fiore, Plotkin and Turi [Fiore et al. 1999],\nL1160@P25: working in the category of presheaves over renamings, (a skeleton of) the category of finite sets.\nL1161@P25: The presheaf condition corresponds to our notion of being Thinnable. Exhibiting algebras based on\nL1162@P25: both de Bruijn level and index encodings, their approach isolates the usual (abstract) arithmetic\nL1163@P25: required of such encodings.\nL1164@P25: By contrast, working in an implemented type theory, where the encoding can be understood as\nL1165@P25: its own foundation, without appeal to an external mathematical semantics, we are able to go further\nL1166@P25: in developing machine-checked such implementations and proofs, themselves generic with respect\nL1167@P25-26: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 2, No. ICFP, Article 90. Publication date: September 2018.A Type and Scope Safe Universe of Syntaxes with Binding 90:27\nL1168@P26: to an abstract syntax Desc of syntaxes-with-binding. Moreover, the usual source of implementation\nL1169@P26: anxiety, namely concrete arithmetic on de Bruijn indices, has been successfully encapsulated via the\nL1170@P26: □ coalgebra structure. It is perhaps noteworthy that our type-theoretic constructions, by contrast\nL1171@P26: with their categorical ones, appear to make fewer commitments as to functoriality, thinnability,\nL1172@P26: etc. in our specification of semantics, with such properties typically being provable as a further\nL1173@P26: instance of our framework.\nL1174@P26: 9.4 Meta-Theory Automation via Tactics and Code Generation\nL1175@P26: The tediousness of repeatedly proving similar statements has unsurprisingly led to various attempts\nL1176@P26: at automating the pain away via either code generation or the definition of tactics. These solutions\nL1177@P26: can be seen as untrusted oracles driving the interactive theorem prover.\nL1178@P26: Polonowski’s DBGen (2013) takes as input a raw syntax with comments annotating binding\nL1179@P26: sites. It generates a module defining lifting, substitution as well as a raw syntax using names and\nL1180@P26: a validation function transforming named terms into de Bruijn ones; we refrain from calling it a\nL1181@P26: scopechecker as terms are not statically proven to be well scoped.\nL1182@P26: Kaiser, Schäfer, and Stark (2018) build on our previous paper to draft possible theoretical foun\u0002dations for Autosubst, a so-far untrusted set of tactics. The paper is based on a specific syntax:\nL1183@P26: well-scoped call-by-value System F. In contrast, our effort has been here to carve out a precise\nL1184@P26: universe of syntaxes with binding and give a systematic account of their semantics and proofs.\nL1185@P26: Keuchel, Weirich, and Schrijvers’ Needle (2016) is a code generator written in Haskell producing\nL1186@P26: syntax-specific Coq modules implementing common traversals and lemmas about them.\nL1187@P26: 9.5 Universes of Syntaxes with Binding\nL1188@P26: Keeping in mind Altenkirch and McBride’s observation that generic programming is everyday\nL1189@P26: programming in dependently-typed languages (2003), we can naturally expect generic, provably\nL1190@P26: sound, treatments of these notions in tools such as Agda or Coq.\nL1191@P26: Keuchel, Weirich, and Schrijvers’ Knot (2016) implements as a set of generic programs the\nL1192@P26: traversals and lemmas generated in specialised forms by their Needle program. They see Needle as\nL1193@P26: a pragmatic choice: working directly with the free monadic terms over finitary containers would\nL1194@P26: be too cumbersome. In our experience solving the POPLMark Reloaded challenge, Agda’s pattern\nL1195@P26: synonyms [Pickering et al. 2016] make working with an encoded definition almost seamless.\nL1196@P26: The GMeta generic framework (2012) provides a universe of syntaxes and offers various binding\nL1197@P26: conventions (locally nameless [Charguéraud 2012] or de Bruijn indices). It also generically imple\u0002ments common traversals (e.g. computing the sets of free variables, shifting de Bruijn indices or\nL1198@P26: substituting terms for parameters) as well as common predicates (e.g. being a closed term) and\nL1199@P26: provides generic lemmas proving that they are well behaved. It does not offer a generic framework\nL1200@P26: for defining new well scoped-and-typed semantics and proving their properties.\nL1201@P26: Érdi (2018) defines a universe inspired by a first draft of this paper and gives three different\nL1202@P26: interpretations (raw, scoped and typed syntax) related via erasure. He provides scope-and-type\nL1203@P26: preserving renaming and substitution as well as various generic proofs that they are well behaved\nL1204@P26: but offers neither a generic notion of semantics, nor generic proof frameworks.\nL1205@P26: Copello (2017) works with named binders and defines nominal techniques (e.g. name swapping)\nL1206@P26: and ultimately α-equivalence over a universe of regular trees with binders inspired by Morris’ (2006).\nL1207@P26: 10 CONCLUSION AND FUTURE WORK\nL1208@P26: Recalling Allais, Chapman, McBride and McKinna’s earlier work (2017) we have started from an\nL1209@P26: example of a scope-and-type safe language (the simply typed λ-calculus), have studied common\nL1210@P26: invariant preserving traversals and noticed their similarity. After introducing a notion of semantics\nL1211@P26-27: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 2, No. ICFP, Article 90. Publication date: September 2018.90:28 G. Allais, R. Atkey, J. Chapman, C. McBride, J. McKinna\nL1212@P27: and refactoring these traversals as instances of the same fundamental lemma, we have observed\nL1213@P27: the tight connection between the abstract definition of semantics and the shape of the language.\nL1214@P27: By extending a universe of datatype descriptions to support a notion of binding, we have given a\nL1215@P27: generic presentation of syntaxes with binding as well as a large class of scope-and-type safe generic\nL1216@P27: programs acting on all of them: from renaming and substitution, to normalisation by evaluation,\nL1217@P27: and the desugaring of new constructors added by a language transformer. The code accompanying\nL1218@P27: the paper also demonstrates how to generically write a printer or a scope-checker elaborating\nL1219@P27: values of a raw syntax using strings as variable names into scope-safe ones.\nL1220@P27: We have seen how to construct generic proofs about these generic programs. We first introduced\nL1221@P27: a Simulation relation showing what it means for two semantics to yield related outputs whenever\nL1222@P27: they are fed related input environments. We then built on our experience to tackle a more involved\nL1223@P27: case: identifying a set of constraints guaranteeing that two semantics run consecutively can be\nL1224@P27: subsumed by a single pass of a third one.\nL1225@P27: We have put all of these results into practice by using them to solve the (to be published)\nL1226@P27: POPLMark Reloaded challenge which consists of formalising strong normalisation for the simply\nL1227@P27: typed λ-calculus via a logical-relation argument. This also gave us the opportunity to try our\nL1228@P27: framework on larger languages by tackling the challenge’s extensions to sum types and Gödel’s\nL1229@P27: System T.\nL1230@P27: Finally, we have demonstrated that this formalisation can be re-used in other domains by seeing\nL1231@P27: our syntaxes with binding as potentially cyclic terms. Their unfolding is a non-standard semantics\nL1232@P27: and we provide the user with a generic notion of bisimilarity to reason about them.\nL1233@P27: The diverse influences leading to this work suggest many opportunities for future research.\nL1234@P27: Our example of the elaboration of an enriched language to a core one, and ACMM’s implementa\u0002tion of a Continuation Passing Style conversion function raises the question of how many such\nL1235@P27: common compilation passes can be implemented generically.\nL1236@P27: An extension of McBride’s theory of ornaments (2017) could provide an appropriate framework to\nL1237@P27: highlight the connection between various languages, some being seen as refinements of others. This\nL1238@P27: is particularly evident when considering the informative typechecker (see the accompanying code)\nL1239@P27: which given a scoped term produces a scoped-and-typed term by type-checking or type-inference.\nL1240@P27: Our work on the POPLMark Reloaded challenge highlights a need for generic notions of congru\u0002ence closure which would come with guarantees (if the original relation is stable under renaming\nL1241@P27: and substitution so should the closure). Similarly, the łevaluation contextsž corresponding to a syn\u0002tax could be derived automatically by building on the work of Huet (1997) and Abbott, Altenkirch,\nL1242@P27: McBride and Ghani (2005), allowing us to revisit previous work based on concrete instances of\nL1243@P27: ACMM such as McLaughlin, McKinna and Stark (2018).\nL1244@P27: Finally, now knowing how to generically describe syntaxes and their well behaved semantics,\nL1245@P27: we can start asking what it means to define well behaved judgments. Why stop at helping the user\nL1246@P27: write their specific language’s meta-theory when we could study meta-meta-theory?\nL1247@P27: ACKNOWLEDGMENTS\nL1248@P27: The research leading to these results has received funding from the European Research Council un\u0002der the European Union’s Seventh Framework Programme (FP7/2007-2013) / ERC grant agreement\nL1249@P27: nr. 320571 and the EPSRC grant EP/M016951/1.\nL1250@P27: REFERENCES\nL1251@P27: Michael Abbott, Thorsten Altenkirch, Conor McBride, and Neil Ghani. 2005. ∂ for data: Differentiating data structures.\nL1252@P27: Fundamenta Informaticae 65, 1-2 (2005), 1ś28.\nL1253@P27-28: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 2, No. ICFP, Article 90. Publication date: September 2018.A Type and Scope Safe Universe of Syntaxes with Binding 90:29\nL1254@P28: Andreas Abel. 2010. MiniAgda: Integrating Sized and Dependent Types. In Proceedings Workshop on Partiality and Recursion\nL1255@P28: in Interactive Theorem Provers, PAR 2010, Edinburgh, UK, 15th July 2010. (EPTCS), Ana Bove, Ekaterina Komendantskaya,\nL1256@P28: and Milad Niqui (Eds.), Vol. 43. 14ś28. DOI:http://dx.doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.43.2\nL1257@P28: Andreas Abel, Alberto Momigliano, and Brigitte Pientka. 2017. POPLMark Reloaded. Proceedings of the Logical Frameworks\nL1258@P28: and Meta-Languages: Theory and Practice Workshop (2017).\nL1259@P28: Andreas Abel, Brigitte Pientka, David Thibodeau, and Anton Setzer. 2013. Copatterns: programming infinite structures by\nL1260@P28: observations. In ACM SIGPLAN Notices, Vol. 48. ACM, 27ś38.\nL1261@P28: Guillaume Allais, James Chapman, Conor McBride, and James McKinna. 2017. Type-and-scope Safe Programs and Their\nL1262@P28: Proofs. In Proceedings of the 6th ACM SIGPLAN Conference on Certified Programs and Proofs (CPP 2017). ACM, 195ś207.\nL1263@P28: DOI:http://dx.doi.org/10.1145/3018610.3018613\nL1264@P28: Thorsten Altenkirch, James Chapman, and Tarmo Uustalu. 2010. Monads Need Not Be Endofunctors. Springer, 297ś311. DOI:\nL1265@P28: http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-12032-9_21\nL1266@P28: Thorsten Altenkirch, James Chapman, and Tarmo Uustalu. 2014. Relative Monads Formalised. Journal of Formalized\nL1267@P28: Reasoning 7, 1 (2014), 1ś43.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1102.2653)\nciteturn98view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1102.2653\",\"lineno\":670}); Total lines: 745\nL585@P12: The datatype TG′ has the same reading as TG, but a simpler structure:\nL586@P12: • If all nodes have been declared, Output as assembles the vector of output nodes.\nL587@P12: • Let x f v u, which, in more conventional notation, would read “let x = f (v) in u”, binds a new node x\nL588@P12: to an edge labelled f with input nodes v, and makes x visible in the remaining term graph suffix u.\nL589@P12: data TG′ E α (m : N) : N → Set where\nL590@P12: Output : {n : N} → Args α m n → TG′ E α m n\nL591@P12: Let : {β : World} {k n : N}\nL592@P12: → (x : E α β) -- let x\nL593@P12: → (f : Label k) (v : Args α m k) -- = f (v)\nL594@P12: → (u : TG′ E β m n) -- in u\nL595@P12: → TG′ E α m n\nL596@P12: We first show that primitive and wiring graphs are easily programmed:\nL597@P12: prim : {k : N} → Label k → TG′ _↼→_ ø k 1\nL598@P12: prim {k} f = Let strongOf f (Vec.map Input (Vec.allFin k)) (Output [V nameOf])\nL599@P12: where open FreshPack freshø\nL600@P12: wire : {k n : N} {E : } {α : World} → Vec (Fin k) n → TG′ E α k n\nL601@P12: wire v = Output (Vec.map Input v)\nL602@P12: idWire : {k : N} {E : } {α : World} → TG′ E α k k\nL603@P12: idWire {k} = wire (Vec.allFin k)\nL604@P12-13: dup : {k : N} {E : } {α : World} → TG′ E α k (k + k)Wolfram Kahl 51\nL605@P13: dup